CAT = 'aufssHygiene'; SEG = 'HYG'
Q = [
('What is the most important way to stop food handlers spreading bacteria?',
 'Washing hands properly', ['Wearing a hair net', 'Wearing a clean apron', 'Using gloves all day long'],
 'Hands are the main way bacteria and viruses reach food. Correct, frequent hand washing is the single most important hygiene control.'),
('What must a hand washing basin in a food premises provide?',
 'Warm running water, soap, towels', ['Cold water and a hand dryer', 'Sanitiser and a cloth towel', 'Hot water and dish detergent'],
 'Standard 3.2.3 requires basins used only for hand washing, with warm running water through a single spout, and Standard 3.2.2 requires soap and single-use towels or another way of drying hands.'),
('A hand basin is full of dirty pots. What is wrong?',
 'It is only for washing hands', ['Pots should soak overnight', 'It needs more detergent', 'It must have cold water only'],
 'Hand wash basins must be used only for washing hands, arms and face, and must be easy to reach. Blocking it stops staff washing their hands when needed.'),
('How long should you wash your hands with soap?',
 'At least 20 seconds', ['About 5 seconds', 'About 2 minutes', 'Until the water runs hot'],
 'Lather all over hands, between fingers and under nails for at least 20 seconds, rinse, then dry with a single-use towel.'),
('Why should hands be dried with single-use paper towels?',
 'Shared towels spread bacteria', ['Paper is cheaper than cloth', 'Air dryers are not allowed', 'Hands should stay slightly wet'],
 'Damp hands transfer bacteria more easily, and shared cloth towels collect them. Single-use towels dry hands well and are thrown away.'),
('When must you wash your hands?',
 'After handling raw meat', ['Only at the start of a shift', 'Only after eating lunch', 'Only when gloves tear'],
 'Wash before starting work, between raw and ready-to-eat food, after the toilet, eating, smoking, touching hair or face, handling rubbish or chemicals, and after cleaning.'),
('Is hand sanitiser a substitute for hand washing in a kitchen?',
 'No, wash with soap and water', ['Yes, at any time', 'Yes, if it is alcohol based', 'Yes, after handling raw meat'],
 'Sanitiser does not remove dirt or grease and does not work well on some viruses. In food areas, wash hands with warm water and soap.'),
('When should you change disposable gloves?',
 'Between raw and cooked food', ['Only at the end of the shift', 'Only when they look dirty', 'Once every two hours'],
 'Gloves carry bacteria just like hands. Change them between tasks, when torn, after touching raw food, rubbish or your face, and wash hands before putting on new ones.'),
('You have a cut on your finger. What should you do before handling food?',
 'Use a waterproof dressing', ['Leave it open to dry out', 'Wrap it in a paper towel', 'Cover it with a cloth bandage'],
 'Cuts can carry Staphylococcus aureus. Cover them with a waterproof, brightly coloured dressing and wear a glove over it for food work.'),
('Why are dressings in kitchens usually brightly coloured?',
 'So they are easy to spot', ['To show who is a first aider', 'To match the uniform colour', 'To keep wounds cooler'],
 'A bright blue or similar dressing is easy to see if it falls into food. Some are also metal-detectable for food factories.'),
('A food handler has diarrhoea. What must they do?',
 'Report it and stop handling food', ['Work but wear two pairs of gloves', 'Work only on dry goods', 'Take medicine and carry on'],
 'Food handlers must tell their supervisor about symptoms such as diarrhoea, vomiting, fever or sore throat with fever, and must not handle food while they could contaminate it.'),
('After vomiting or diarrhoea stops, how long is a handler usually kept off food work?',
 '48 hours', ['2 hours', '12 hours', '7 days'],
 'Safe Food Australia guidance is to exclude handlers until 48 hours after symptoms of vomiting or diarrhoea stop, because they can still carry the virus or bacteria.'),
('Which symptom must a food handler report to their supervisor?',
 'Fever with a sore throat', ['Tired, dry eyes', 'A mild tension headache', 'Sore feet after a shift'],
 'Food handlers must report symptoms of a foodborne illness, such as diarrhoea, vomiting, fever, sore throat with fever and jaundice, and infected skin lesions.'),
('Why must food handlers report if they have hepatitis A?',
 'They can contaminate food', ['They need extra breaks', 'It affects their uniform', 'It causes food allergies'],
 'Food handlers must report if they are known or suspected to carry a foodborne disease such as hepatitis A. They must not handle food while they could pass it on.'),
('What does Standard 3.2.2 say about clothing for food handlers?',
 'It must be clean', ['It must be white', 'It must be new each day', 'It must be paper'],
 'Food handlers must wear clean outer clothing suited to their work. Colours and uniform styles are a business choice.'),
('Why should food handlers tie back or cover long hair?',
 'To keep hair out of food', ['To keep their hair cooler', 'To meet the dress code only', 'To hide the hair colour'],
 'Hair can fall into food as a physical hazard and can carry bacteria. Tie it back and cover it, and avoid touching it while working.'),
('Which jewellery is usually acceptable for food handlers?',
 'A plain band ring', ['A stone-set ring', 'Large hoop earrings', 'A charm bracelet'],
 'Jewellery can trap bacteria and fall into food. Many businesses allow only a plain band ring and sleeper earrings; check your workplace policy.'),
('Why should food handlers keep fingernails short and unpolished?',
 'Dirt and polish can get in food', ['To type orders faster', 'So gloves fit much better', 'To meet a workplace fashion rule'],
 'Long nails trap dirt and bacteria, and polish or false nails can flake into food. Short, clean nails are easier to scrub.'),
('A food handler sneezes over a bench of uncovered desserts. What is the problem?',
 'It can spread bacteria', ['It is only a quality issue', 'Sneezing kills bacteria', 'Desserts are not high risk'],
 'Standard 3.2.2 says food handlers must not sneeze, blow or cough over unprotected food or surfaces likely to touch food. Turn away and wash hands after.'),
('Where may food handlers eat during their shift?',
 'Away from food areas', ['At the prep bench if quick', 'In the cool room', 'Over the dish sink'],
 'Food handlers must not eat over unprotected food or food contact surfaces. Take breaks in a separate area and wash hands before returning.'),
('What must a smoker do before going back to food handling?',
 'Wash their hands', ['Change their apron only', 'Use mouthwash only', 'Put on a hair net'],
 'Smoking is banned in food handling areas. Fingers touch the lips while smoking, so handlers must wash hands before returning to food.'),
('Why should you not touch ready-to-eat food with bare hands?',
 'It will not be cooked again', ['It wastes food', "It damages the food's colour", 'It is against tipping rules'],
 'Bacteria on hands transfer to ready-to-eat food, and no cooking step will kill them. Use tongs, utensils or clean gloves.'),
('How should you taste food while cooking?',
 'With a clean spoon each time', ['With a clean finger', 'From the stirring spoon', 'Straight from the serving ladle'],
 'Use a clean spoon for each taste and do not put it back in the food. Finger tasting moves saliva and bacteria into the food.'),
('What should staff do with personal items like phones and bags?',
 'Keep them out of food areas', ['Put them on shelves near food', 'Keep them in aprons', 'Store them in the cool room'],
 'Personal items carry dirt and bacteria and can fall into food. Store them in lockers or a staff area away from food handling.'),
('Why should staff not wear uniforms to travel to work?',
 'Clothes collect dirt outside', ['Uniforms must last longer', 'It breaks company branding', 'Uniforms are too warm'],
 'Outside clothing picks up dirt, hair and bacteria. Change into clean work clothes at work so you do not bring contamination into the kitchen.'),
('Who must a food handler tell if they notice a hygiene breach?',
 'Their supervisor', ['The customer', 'A colleague only', 'No one, fix it quietly'],
 'SITXFSA005 asks handlers to report unsafe practices promptly so the supervisor can fix them and stop them happening again.'),
('A worker returns after a stomach bug. What should they do first?',
 'Wash hands before starting', ['Start work without washing', 'Work only on raw meat', 'Skip gloves for comfort'],
 'Once cleared to return, they must follow strict hand washing, especially after using the toilet. Viruses can still be shed after symptoms stop.'),
('Can a food handler wipe their hands on their apron?',
 'No, use a single-use towel', ['Yes, if the apron is clean', 'Yes, between tasks only', 'Yes, if gloves are worn'],
 'Wiping hands on an apron or tea towel spreads bacteria. Dry hands only with single-use towels or a hand dryer.'),
('Which practice is a breach of personal hygiene?',
 'Licking fingers to open bags', ['Covering a cut with a plaster', 'Tying back long hair', 'Removing a watch before work'],
 'Licking fingers transfers saliva and bacteria to hands and food. Use a clean method to open bags or separate sheets.'),
('Must food handlers wash their hands after touching their face or hair?',
 'Yes, every time', ['Only if it looked dirty', 'Only after their hair', 'No, gloves cover it'],
 'Hair, nose and mouth carry bacteria such as Staphylococcus aureus. Wash hands each time you touch them before handling food again.'),
('When should you wash your hands after handling rubbish?',
 'Straight away', ['At the end of the shift', 'Only before eating', 'Only if visibly dirty'],
 'Rubbish carries high levels of bacteria. Wash your hands straight after handling bins or waste and before touching food.'),
('What should a supervisor do if a worker has a weeping skin infection on their hand?',
 'Remove them from food work', ['Give them thinner gloves', 'Let them handle only salads', 'Ask them to wash more often'],
 'Infected skin lesions can carry Staphylococcus aureus. A handler with an infected sore that cannot be fully covered should not handle food.'),
]
